        "body": "Component 3 paired review of #179: approved.\n\nChecked at 987d50a in a clean worktree:\n- `go build`, `go vet` and `go test ./...` pass.\n- `contracts/validate.py`: 235 invariant checks pass. `contracts` unit tests: 26 pass.\n- Re-ran `scripts/run_issue172_policy.py` with a binary I built myself. Results match the PR: copied-function, destructured-copy, direct-unimported-b and transitive-b give `unknown`; direct-positive-b gives `usage_detected`; observed-other-symbol gives `no_usage_detected`.\n\nCode read:\n- `DetermineState` keeps matched positive evidence first and only then refuses a negative for an occurrence known only by absence of an application import. The justification gets one extra reason, so the downgrade is explained.\n- `validate.py` now blocks a negative for any unanalysed entry on the same occurrence or the same purl. `purl` is required on findings, so the purl comparison cannot match two missing values.\n- `negativeEligible` is now only observed occurrences. This is stricter than before and is the right direction for C3: a finding without a closed candidate set or observed import can no longer turn into a VEX negative.\n\nNotes, none blocking:\n- observed-other-symbol can still give a scoped `no_usage_detected`. The PR says so and README/architecture record it as a residual limitation. I agree to leave it.\n- I did not re-run lint, the 35 script tests or the original-baseline regression; I rely on the PR's CI (green) and the evidence in `docs/evidence/issue172/run-01/` for those.\n\nC3 effect: the VEX exporter will now write `under_investigation` for the direct-but-unimported case, which is the conservative result we want.\n",
        "state": "APPROVED",
